---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Projects">
  <h1>Research Projects</h1>
  <p>A look at the research I've worked on, from graduate school back through my earliest lab experiences.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Dr. Kevin Healy Lab, UC Berkeley</h3>

    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-bottom: 0.2rem;">
      Hyaluronic Acid Hydrogels for Soft Tissue Regeneration
    </p>
    <p>
      I engineer in situ, semi-synthetic hyaluronic acid hydrogels designed
      to help the body regenerate tissue after complex soft tissue
      injuries, such as rotator cuff tears. The work draws on hydrogel
      engineering, biochemistry, and cell-material interactions to design a
      therapeutic that's effective, safe, and accessible&mdash;regardless
      of a patient's biology or geography. As an inventor on MuscleMatrix,
      I've helped move this research from the bench toward something that
      could reach the clinic.
    </p>
    <div class="home-cta">
      <a
        class="btn secondary"
        href="https://biomaterials.berkeley.edu/"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >
        Visit the Healy Lab
      </a>
      <a
        class="btn secondary"
        href="https://ipira.berkeley.edu/musclematrix"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >
        Learn more about MuscleMatrix
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Directed Assembly of Particles and Suspensions (DAPS) Lab<br />Dr. Randall Erb, Northeastern University</h3>

    <img
      src="/daps-hydrogel.jpg"
      alt="A dual-network hydrogel sample held with forceps in a gloved hand"
      class="card-img"
    />
    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-bottom: 0.2rem;">
      Dual-Network Hydrogels with Functionalized Hydroxyapatite Rods for Bone Grafts
    </p>
    <p>
      I developed a protocol to fabricate dual-network hydrogels reinforced
      with magnetically responsive hydroxyapatite fibers for bone graft
      applications, tailoring the composite design to maximize toughness. I
      characterized the mechanical strength of these materials using
      scanning electron microscopy and dynamic mechanical analysis.
    </p>

    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-top: 1.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.2rem; padding-top: 1.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);">
      Computational Modeling of Hydroxyapatite Rod Alignment &amp; Toughening Mechanisms
    </p>
    <!-- Add photo: /daps-rod-orientation.jpg -->
    <p>
      In a computational extension of this work, I ran COMSOL Multiphysics
      simulations of hydroxyapatite-reinforced alginate/gelatin hydrogels
      to investigate how the alignment of the rods in different
      orientations affects the composite's toughness and how stress is
      transduced through the material&mdash;a potential new toughening
      mechanism. This project won the RISE Excellence in Research Award.
    </p>

    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-top: 1.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.2rem; padding-top: 1.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);">
      Freeze-Casted Nerve Repair Conduits
    </p>
    <!-- Add photo: /daps-nerve-conduit.jpg -->
    <p>
      In collaboration with Dartmouth College and Harvard Medical School, I
      manufactured freeze-casted, double-layered porous conduits for
      peripheral nerve repair, and wrote a successful grant proposal that
      funded this work through the PEAK Summit Award.
    </p>

    <a
      class="btn secondary"
      href="https://www.dapslab.com/"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      Visit the DAPS Lab
    </a>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Vertex Pharmaceuticals</h3>

    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-bottom: 0.2rem;">
      Druggable Pathways for Sickle Cell Disease
    </p>
    <p>
      As an Exploratory Research Co-op at Vertex, I developed and optimized
      cellular assays to detect transcription factors in potential
      druggable pathways for the treatment of Sickle Cell Disease, running
      pilot experiments to track protein expression profiles over time. I
      used molecular cloning of recombinant vectors to achieve stable
      integration into cell lines of interest, and regularly presented my
      findings and relevant literature to the Exploratory Research group
      and the broader SCD team.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Editas Medicine</h3>

    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-bottom: 0.2rem;">
      CRISPR Screening &amp; Automation
    </p>
    <p>
      In the Screening Group at Editas Medicine, I screened for the most
      effective sgRNAs across the company's pipeline diseases and
      performed CRISPR-mediated mutations via nucleofection, transfection,
      and viral transduction. I programmed and optimized high-throughput
      screening assays for automated liquid handlers, ran a full
      comparative analysis of the company's Biomek i7 and Biomek FX
      platforms, and used tools like Prism, Tableau, and FlowJo to analyze
      and present data across the screening and broader research
      departments.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Dr. Marc Hansen Lab, UCONN Health</h3>

    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-bottom: 0.2rem;">
      Synthetic-Lethal Targets in Osteosarcoma
    </p>
    <!-- Add photo: /uconn-synthetic-lethal.jpg -->
    <p>
      As a Summer Research Fellow, I piloted synthetic-lethal experiments to
      uncover exploitable vulnerabilities in cell signaling pathways and
      identify novel druggable targets in osteosarcoma. I tested inhibitor
      and siRNA combinations on undifferentiated hFOB cells and
      osteosarcoma cell lines (SaOS2 and U2OS) to selectively target tumor
      cells with growth-factor-receptor mutations, using sterile cell
      culture technique alongside PCR and absorbance assays. This work
      resulted in a poster presentation and laid the foundation for the
      lab's subsequent NIH grant proposals.
    </p>

    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-top: 1.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.2rem; padding-top: 1.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);">
      Laser Capture Microdissection in Paget's Disease of Bone
    </p>
    <!-- Add photo: /uconn-laser-capture.jpg -->
    <p>
      In my second summer with the lab, I used laser capture microdissection
      on histological sections to investigate osteoblast and osteoclast
      cell behavior and genetics in Paget's disease of bone&mdash;work made
      possible by isolating specific cell populations directly from tissue
      sections using the laser capture technique.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
